WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.960
RENEE H.: I was a
child during the war

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.500
when, the Germans came
into Czechoslovakia.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.280
I was not yet six years
old, and at that time,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.470
I was living in Brno, which
was the third largest city.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.660
Things were not explained to me.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.490
Children at that time
were kept in ignorance

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.560
of much of what was going
on, politically or otherwise,

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:44.740
and only when it impinged
on actual family events

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.640
were some explanations given.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.930
I have one recollection
of the German presence

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:56.100
of that time, which was
when Hitler supposedly

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.580
went through the town and I
do remember my father saying

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.430
that I was not to
go in the streets.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:08.070
I first remember seeing
German soldiers just walking

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.850
on the street, nothing
happened much in the beginning.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.500
But slowly there was
abuse, and the abuse

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.750
came not so much
from the Germans

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.100
alone as it came from
the native population,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.830
the Slovaks themselves.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:29.880
I remember coming home one day
and seeing my mother sewing

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:37.390
on the star on my coat and
I was aware of the fact

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:41.030
that this was a way
of singling us out

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.620
and I remember saying,
"Let's not do this

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.980
because then we can't hide."

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:54.160
And she said, "We have
to, that is the decree."

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.270
And what happened
in the town itself

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:01.930
was the closing in
of the neighborhood.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.750
The Jews, we did
not have a ghetto,

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:09.160
and yet there was a
ghetto atmosphere there.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.580
Because what happened is that
certain neighborhoods where

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.400
Jews lived which were more
elegant neighborhoods they

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.780
were told they had to leave.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.160
They could not stay.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.090
So they had to move into a
smaller and smaller enclave,

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:25.980
the main street of
which was Judengasse.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.870
And I had always been a child
that used to roam around,

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.570
to my parents despair.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.910
But so I got to know
the city terribly well.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.290
And what I would
do in order to be

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.710
able to continue that sense
of freedom that I had,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.260
would be constantly
to try to wear a scarf

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:47.340
to cover up the
Jewish star, and I

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.420
would pretend that if anybody
addressed me in some way

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.290
I wouldn't understand
what they were saying.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:01.560
But I did feel that
every exit into the town

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.680
outside of my immediate
home was into a very

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.130
hostile and dangerous
environment.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.010
That I always could
be found out and I

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.550
would have fantasies about
what would happen to me if they

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:18.210
found me out, and it
was always accompanied

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:22.366
by my immediately running
back home, to touch base.

00:03:22.366 --> 00:03:24.505
INTERVIEWER 1:
What would happen?

00:03:24.505 --> 00:03:28.790
RENEE H.: I, the
worst that I ever saw

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:33.470
anybody was to get a
very thorough beating,

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:40.100
and curiously enough I
always expected the beating,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.530
but I never received it.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:48.130
I never could understand
why, and one day, I

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:51.820
do remember asking
a teacher whom

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.180
I knew who lived in
the neighborhood,

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.600
as to why everybody left me
alone even though they saw me

00:03:58.600 --> 00:03:59.910
on the street.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:05.060
And she said to me, "That's
because you have blonde hair."

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.480
Somehow, that
seemed to me to make

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.990
me a little more acceptable.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:14.810
But I was aware
of the fact that I

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:20.930
didn't want to identify
with the Slovaks,

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:23.270
and yet I wanted the
freedom of the town which

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:24.950
I had gotten so used to.

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.020
And I wanted to know
what was going on,

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.900
because my parents
weren't telling me much,

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.720
and I wanted to
explore and sense

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.890
what really was happening.

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:37.960
And I,

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:41.450
But the worst parts
for me at the time

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.460
was watching the transports.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.260
And the transport would
just suddenly appear,

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:56.220
and I remember that we would
hear certain sounds of boots

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.290
on the street, and
usually, whenever

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.660
there was a transport
it was accompanied

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.380
by 10 to 12 soldiers
coming, marching together

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:08.665
from house to house, and
gathering all the people

00:05:08.665 --> 00:05:14.530
and knocking on the doors and
saying, "You have an hour."

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:18.730
People had only an hour in
which they could gather families

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:20.560
together.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.770
Often, what would happen is
if the whole family was not

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:28.330
together, a child would be
sent out to bring in the rest,

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:31.110
try to, and that
used to horrify me

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.970
because there would
sometimes be a child who

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:39.160
would come running back crying,
"I don't know where Papa is."

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:46.990
And so, what happened was that
there was all these transports

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.690
would get together
on the street,

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:53.580
and the people would
wait there, hoping

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.420
that the family, the rest
of the family would come.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.780
And eventually what happened
is that, which I did not

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.250
know because no one ever
told me those things,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:05.730
and it was not possible
to ask questions

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.010
because every time
you asked a question,

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.320
they just give you a slap.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.860
And what I did find out was
that eventually families

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.810
were united at a central
post, because when

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.510
the rest of the family came
home and asked where they were,

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:23.430
they would rush to
the police station

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:28.400
and immediately give
themselves up to the Gestapo.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:35.030
And in 1943, my family
decided that they

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.650
had to save part of the family.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.960
They saw these
transports coming,

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:43.220
and it was just a
monthly occurrence

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:48.570
and there would be 2,000 people
sometimes in the transports.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:51.390
Sometimes there would
be considerably less.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.620
We used to live in
terror of these boots

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.080
and we lived on the fourth
floor in the apartment

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.800
and my parents were both
deaf, and I had a deaf sister,

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:03.050
so I became the ears.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:07.800
I would have to warn them
that the transport was coming.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.490
And what I would do is we
would all rush in the back room

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.630
and when they knocked on
the door, we didn't answer.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:17.370
We were in the back
room just trying

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.410
to be as quiet as possible

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:22.820
and what happened is
that as a result of this,

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:28.460
the responsibility was on me to
always hear what was going on,

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:32.810
because any time I heard
10 boots marching down

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.300
around the corner I would
run immediately home

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:40.150
and tell everybody to
go into the apartment.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:45.190
And, sometimes we were
together, sometimes we were not.

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:51.990
And we used to pray that nobody
would be coming up the stairs

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.620
just at the same time.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:08:00.320
And it got so that we all got to
be very, very good in spotting.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.270
What happened is
that we developed

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:06.290
eyes that looked for
everything, and I was the ears.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:08.210
And so in the
middle of the night

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:12.060
sometimes people would
come up the stairs,

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.470
but there were no transport
collections at night,

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.710
but they would be what they
called, "neighborhood spies."

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:22.010
People who tried to find
out whether the family

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.420
was still there and so on.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.990
And we had to watch
out for that too,

00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:31.400
and I ended up so that I
was constantly listening

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:35.049
and I was able to
identify sounds.

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:41.320
And, but in, by 1943,
my parents realized

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:47.800
that it was just hopeless, that
there was no point in trying

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.470
to live in this fearful way.

00:08:50.470 --> 00:08:56.170
And so they decided to send my
sister and me to a farm family

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:00.040
out in the country, for
which they paid great sums

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:04.330
and I had to hide the
fact that I was Jewish.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:07.660
And I remember my mother taking
all the stars off my clothes

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:11.020
again and sending me off.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.210
And this was in the s--

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:18.850
I would say around the
spring or the summer of 1943.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:24.220
I would, I was taken by
a person who was also

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:27.880
deaf to his mother's
home in the countryside,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:34.000
somewhere in the, near the Tatra
Mountains, at the foothills.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:38.860
And I was introduced as a very
distant relationship whose

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.885
parents were in the
hospital and sick

00:09:40.885 --> 00:09:44.980
and I had to be taken care of
for a certain amount of time

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:48.010
until I got the
message to come back.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:52.250
I was told never to tell
anyone I was Jewish.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:55.750
I was told that even though
I didn't have to go to church

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:59.080
that I had to pretend that I...

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:01.840
everything Christian
was very familiar to me,

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.930
and very desired by me.

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:07.750
So that, for example,
if the priest went by

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:08.900
I had to cross myself.

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.680
It used to be terrible
to do that for me

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.870
because I felt that I
was going to in some way

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:20.470
have to pay for this,
but I nevertheless

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:22.200
knew that I had
my sister as well

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.340
to take care of, who was a year
and a half younger than myself.

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:33.130
And I never saw my
parents again afterwards.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:39.040
In the spring of 1944, the
son came back to the farm

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:44.890
and announced to me that
my parents had failed

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:49.600
to pay for the last five
months of my stay there,

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:53.890
and consequently somebody else
was going to take care of us

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.080
in Bratislava itself.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.330
So when we went
back to Bratislava,

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:03.220
there were various people
we knew who had papers,

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:08.440
forged papers, and they told
us that it would be impossible

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:10.660
for us to have
the forged papers.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:14.350
When we said, "Where
are our parents?"

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:16.885
They said they didn't know,
but they were still in hiding.

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:21.660
They did never,
they never told us

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:25.230
that they had already been
taken by the last transport out

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.150
of Bratislava.

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:33.220
And finally I
remember one evening,

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:37.270
hiding on the third floor of
a building which was really

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:42.640
the place where a tailor
kept his workshop.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:46.120
And we had to stay out
of the place all day

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:48.820
long because he
had workmen there,

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:52.030
and as he explained to us that
those, many of those workmen

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:54.360
were very Antisemitic.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:56.710
And if they knew
that we were there,

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:59.860
spending the night
in the dark, they

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:02.850
would betray us without a doubt.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:07.360
And so I remember spending
the days, for three weeks,

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:13.410
on the streets, going into
shops when I had to go in,

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:17.190
going into the church,
going all over the place.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:21.930
I remember one episode
which was very harrowing,

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:26.770
was when somebody who knew
my parents recognized me,

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:29.790
and it was rather easy to
recognize me at that time

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:32.420
because I had very blond,
very tightly curled

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:36.120
hair, sort of like
an Afro in blond.

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:39.510
And he immediately
says, "come here,

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.520
I'm going to take care of you."

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:46.800
And I knew immediately that
I just could not trust him.

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.440
In fact, my, I had lost
trust in everybody.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:55.170
I was very suspicious and very
careful to watch and listen

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:58.900
for any force or
difficult notes,

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.080
and I said, "Never, I
wasn't going to come."

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:05.030
And I started to run
and he chased me,

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:09.050
and it was a very good thing
that my knowledge of Bratislava

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:13.460
came to my aid because
I was able to weave

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:15.980
my way around
streets and corners

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:20.600
and little openings in
buildings and finally I

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.720
managed to lose him.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:26.720
I noticed one thing when
I came back to Bratislava,

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.450
that there were no
more transports,

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:31.190
there were no more gatherings.

00:13:31.190 --> 00:13:33.380
And the transports
used to terrify me

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:37.740
because the speed of them was
so horrendous where they were

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:41.780
trying to force, they
would yell at the people

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:43.550
and say, "get out.

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:44.090
Get out.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:45.410
Quickly, get out.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:46.400
Gather together.

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:47.870
You can't take this suitcase.

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:49.200
You can take this suitcase.

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:51.020
Only a little bit with you.

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:52.850
No, you mustn't
take this blanket."

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:57.230
And they would just
force the blanket away.

00:13:57.230 --> 00:14:03.170
In the process also there would
be a lot of hitting, especially

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:07.190
of old people, and of
sick people and children,

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:13.220
and the shoving and pushing,
and those gatherings of people

00:14:13.220 --> 00:14:14.480
just terrified me.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:18.980
I really, it was
a way of behaving

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:24.020
which I could never
experience except in, not

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:25.130
even in nightmares.

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:29.960
I even remember my nightmares
as being mild compared to this.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:33.160
But what was terrible
is that it was

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.450
the behavior towards
the very people

00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:39.430
that the Jewish
religion says must

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:41.230
have a great deal
of kindness, who

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:44.200
are the sick, the
old, and the children.

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:48.760
And I saw everything
being done which

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:54.520
was opposed to what I believed
in and what I had grown up in.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:58.120
And I remember when I
came back to Bratislava,

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:01.450
one of the things I noticed that
those transports had ceased.

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:06.400
So it was really, in one sense,
a relief, but in another sense

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.980
a terribly scary thing
because I suddenly

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:13.390
realized that I might be the
last, one of the few last Jews

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:17.440
left in the town, that
they all had been taken

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:19.420
except for the
three or four people

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:23.060
I knew who were
on forged papers.

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:27.650
And the thought of being one
of the last Jews was terrible.

00:15:27.650 --> 00:15:28.790
I couldn't bear it.

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:31.830
I kept thinking, "no, I don't
want to be the last one alive."

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:39.010
And I must have realized
that many of them

00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:41.415
went to death
because otherwise I

00:15:41.415 --> 00:15:42.790
wouldn't have said
that, "I don't

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:44.110
want to be the last one alive."

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:51.290
And finally, after three weeks
of living on the streets,

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:54.230
being terrified of the thought
of meeting the man who had

00:15:54.230 --> 00:16:00.560
chased me again, being terrified
of meeting anyone who knew me

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:01.130
as a Jew.

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:04.010
I had been away from the
town for about nine months,

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:08.990
so I had hoped that somehow
I, in growing I had somehow

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:12.420
become less noticeable.

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:14.580
But after three weeks
of living like that,

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.610
the man said the people
had gotten suspicious

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:21.720
because they found bread crumbs,
and they were complaining

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:25.620
and they thought that the
owner of this workshop

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:28.320
had harbored some fugitives.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:32.480
And so he finally told
me he couldn't keep me.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:36.660
I had to find some
other accommodation.

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:41.150
And I took my suitcase and
my sister looking for a place

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:43.020
to stay.

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.120
And I would go to
places where I knew

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:47.410
people who were Christians.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:51.100
I knocked on the
door and they slammed

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:56.910
the door right in my face
and not saying a word,

00:16:56.910 --> 00:17:03.310
not explaining, just as if I
were just a beggar at the door

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:06.509
and they just closed
the door in my face.

00:17:06.509 --> 00:17:11.690
And finally I went to
the home of these friends

00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:15.540
took my parents who were
living on a forged passport,

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:18.099
and they did the same thing.

00:17:18.099 --> 00:17:22.928
And then I knew indeed that
I was the last Jew left.

00:17:22.928 --> 00:17:24.220
There was nothing for me to do.

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:26.700
For two days, we
were scrounging.

00:17:26.700 --> 00:17:28.410
We had no money.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:33.250
We were scrounging around,
trying to find food

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:39.440
and finally I said to my
sister, "we can't live that way,

00:17:39.440 --> 00:17:40.890
we will die on the street.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:43.830
So we might as well
just go to the police."

00:17:43.830 --> 00:17:50.470
And I went to the police
and I said my name

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:53.020
and I said my
parents' name, and I

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:56.670
said I would like to join them.

00:17:56.670 --> 00:18:01.420
And the police were amused.

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:02.980
They first laughed,
and then they

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:06.560
realized that I was
perfectly serious.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:07.160
And they...

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:11.360
suddenly realized that I had
presented them with a problem.

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:13.780
They didn't know
what to do with me.

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:16.450
They didn't know what
to do, so they told me

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:20.470
that I could sit on the
bench and I spent the night

00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:22.450
in the police
station on the bench

00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:25.920
while they were trying to
find out what to do with me.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:29.720
They didn't explain
anything to me.

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:32.270
One of the policemen,
I remember,

00:18:32.270 --> 00:18:38.450
said, "well, we are trying to
find where your parents are."

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:43.190
And they finally must
have found out somehow

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:47.030
because I was immediately
put on a truck

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:50.810
and driven into
Hungary, and that

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:52.670
was in Sered, which
was a gathering

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:58.190
point for the last remaining
transports coming out

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:00.710
of Bratislava because
nothing was going directly

00:19:00.710 --> 00:19:02.480
from Bratislava.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:07.190
I arrived there to discover
that two weeks before my parents

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:09.740
had been put on the
train to Auschwitz.

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:12.200
This was the first time
I heard about Auschwitz.

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:14.410
I didn't know anything about it.

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:22.090
And I was finally put on one
train, and it was a cattle car

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:25.390
and I, in some ways,
was terribly relieved

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:28.330
to be there because here
I was among Jews again.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:30.940
They were all
strangers, but still

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:34.550
the fact that they were Jews
was very important to me.

00:19:37.924 --> 00:19:40.950
INTERVIEWER 2: Say how old
you were during all this time.

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:44.730
RENEE H.: I was, at that
time I was nine years old.

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:50.910
And I had my sister who
was seven and a half,

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:54.690
and to whom I had to
explain everything,

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:59.580
who had to be basically taken
care of because she only knew

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:02.720
the sign language and
I could sign with her,

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:06.110
but she didn't know
how to communicate yet

00:20:06.110 --> 00:20:09.210
with the hearing people.

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:14.670
And so I was really constantly
having to hold her by her hand

00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:18.840
so that, because I couldn't call
her, I couldn't do anything.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:21.510
I couldn't ever
lose sight of her

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:23.830
because in the
milling of the people

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:27.900
we could have been separated.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:30.510
And we finally were
put on the train,

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:34.850
and I was told that I would
be going to Auschwitz.

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:37.820
And to me, of course,
it was just an image

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:41.420
of my rejoining my parents.

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:45.640
We were on the
train for five days

00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:48.490
and during the whole
process of our trip,

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:52.750
we would have to stop because
there was bombing going on.

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:56.860
Sometimes the bombs sounded
as if they were very close,

00:20:56.860 --> 00:21:00.730
and we thought that they
would get the train.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:08.580
And we were I would say
maybe 300 people in each car.

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:13.520
And my sister and I sat on the
suitcase the whole five days

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:16.290
that we were in the train.

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:19.170
And at one point the
bombing, at one point

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:22.590
sounded as if it were the
front of the car had been hit.

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:29.440
It was so loud, and the car
itself jumped the tracks

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:34.720
and we were stuck there for
what seemed to me forever.

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:41.560
And one day, as we were
sort of sitting in the car,

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:45.610
we were told that we would
not be going to Auschwitz,

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:48.146
that we would be re-routed.

00:21:48.146 --> 00:21:51.310
And I remember having
to get out of the car

00:21:51.310 --> 00:21:53.530
because it had jumped the
tracks and there was no way

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:58.990
for it to move and crossing the
tracks, going into another car,

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:03.670
and spending another
three days in the tracks.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:06.740
They did, the Germans
did provide food for us,

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:08.040
but it was so minimal.

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:12.260
It was just a slice, two or
three slices of bread and water

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:14.170
and that was all.

00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:19.690
And, uh, some of
us were so weak we

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:23.050
could barely make it across
the tracks to the other.

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:27.410
So we had to help each other
and for some strange reason

00:22:27.410 --> 00:22:30.260
my sister and I, maybe
because we were so young,

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:33.760
we were, somehow
had managed to get

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:39.850
through the ordeal of
being eight days cooped up

00:22:39.850 --> 00:22:43.480
in a very tight space.

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:49.510
And after about three days,
we arrived in Bergen-Belsen.

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:53.080
And then there was a long march.

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:55.930
And the march was the
most horrendous thing

00:22:55.930 --> 00:22:59.540
I have ever experienced
because after eight days,

00:22:59.540 --> 00:23:02.590
the people just were
in no shape to walk

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:09.490
from the station to the
reception house at the camp.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:13.150
And there, that march
which was, seemed endless.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:19.270
It was, it looked enormously
long, maybe in my mind

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:21.070
now, it seems
almost three hours,

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.485
but it couldn't have been.

00:23:23.485 --> 00:23:27.540
But there we would slowly
walk and the Germans

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:34.290
kept shouting and yelling and
there were truncheons and dogs

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:37.530
and they would
fall on the people,

00:23:37.530 --> 00:23:39.615
asking them to hurry and hurry.

00:23:39.615 --> 00:23:42.070
And I remember the first
night falling asleep.

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:47.340
I had nothing to eat for a long,
long time, nothing to drink,

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:55.750
but I fell asleep, and
I remember thinking,

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:57.850
life had played a
terrible trick on me

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:01.360
because I wasn't in
Auschwitz with my parents.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.630
And I remember saying to
myself as I fell asleep,

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:06.790
"I will never stop
looking for them."

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.680
When we came in with the
transport to Bergen-Belsen,

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:19.110
the separation occurred
in the train station

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:22.470
already, so that when
we started our march,

00:24:22.470 --> 00:24:27.210
I already noticed that there
were no men around and no boys.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:34.060
And when I came to look
at those transports, um,

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:36.730
I noticed that one
of the soldiers who

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:38.980
used to be very
irritated with me

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:41.860
all the time, especially
when I started

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:44.650
to ask in perfect
German, "could I

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:48.550
please call out
my mother's name?"

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:49.990
I knew my mother
couldn't hear me

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:53.170
but I thought maybe somebody
who knew that she was deaf

00:24:53.170 --> 00:24:56.130
would tell her that I had
been calling out her name.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:25:01.490
And I would call out her
name over and over again.

00:25:01.490 --> 00:25:08.460
By doing that, one day for
about the fifth or sixth time,

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:11.430
the soldier so lost his
temper, he just picked me up

00:25:11.430 --> 00:25:14.610
and flung me against a stone.

00:25:14.610 --> 00:25:18.990
And I lost consciousness
at that time.

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:23.520
And the immediate result
was that I lost for,

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:30.600
temporarily my hearing,
which was in some way to me

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:34.260
a sense of tremendous
worry because I

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:36.090
had to be my sister's ears.

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:37.980
I had to hear for her.

00:25:37.980 --> 00:25:40.890
And I was afraid
that I had really had

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:44.070
my hearing permanently damaged.

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:47.940
But after about five or six
days, my hearing came back.

00:25:47.940 --> 00:25:53.860
Later on, in
Bergen-Belsen, I realized

00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:58.690
that my sister was being
watched by the camp doctor.

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:00.040
I do not know who it was.

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:03.150
He was a German
doctor, but he would

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:05.890
come by the children's
barracks all the time,

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:09.850
come to my sister, pinch
her cheeks, pull her ears,

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:13.210
and try to be very
friendly with her.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:19.510
And then, one day he
said to me that he

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:22.570
would be able to give us
oranges and chocolates

00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:27.220
if I allowed my sister
to go into the hospital

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:30.620
for a few days.

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:33.200
And my sister was
perfectly well,

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:37.010
so I couldn't understand why
he wanted her in the hospital

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:41.990
and I just was very sassy
and I said, "no, you are not,

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:46.210
because if you are,
I'm going to kick you."

00:26:46.210 --> 00:26:52.090
And I went up and sort of
said, I am really going to,

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:54.580
I'm serious, I mean it.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:57.910
And so he laughed and
then forgot about it.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:26:59.860
Later on, the
Blockalteste told me

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:04.270
that he had hoped to be
able to use my sister

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:07.480
for scientific research.

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:10.060
Right across from us
was a charnel house

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:14.900
filled with corpses,
not just inside

00:27:14.900 --> 00:27:16.390
but overflowing all over.

00:27:16.390 --> 00:27:18.920
There were corpses all over.

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:23.120
I lived, walked
beside dead people

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:30.010
and after a while, it just
got to be so that one noticed

00:27:30.010 --> 00:27:33.150
and one had to say
to oneself, "I'm

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:35.550
not going to see who it is.

00:27:35.550 --> 00:27:37.620
I'm not going to
recognize anyone

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:41.850
in this person who
is lying there."

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:45.630
And, it got to the
point where I realized

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:50.950
that I had to close my
eyes to a number of things.

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:55.090
Otherwise, I would not have
survived even at that time

00:27:55.090 --> 00:27:58.970
because I saw people
around me going mad.

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:02.210
I was not only having to live
with all of these things,

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:04.070
but with madness.

00:28:04.070 --> 00:28:09.020
One thing I did receive
from the woman in Sered

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:12.230
was a small bag
of tea which I was

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:14.600
able during the time
of the typhus epidemic

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:19.580
to exchange for a
vaccine for my sister,

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:22.550
and I was told it
was a typhus vaccine.

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:24.930
Whether it was one
or not, I'm not sure,

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:27.980
but the fact is that my sister
never did get the typhus.

00:28:27.980 --> 00:28:29.390
I did

00:28:29.390 --> 00:28:33.980
and so one of the
saddest thing in my life

00:28:33.980 --> 00:28:38.300
has been that I have no
recollection of the liberation

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:42.290
because I was totally
ill with the typhus.

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:46.350
I have no recollection of what
happened when the English came

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:47.960
to Bergen-Belsen.

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:49.630
I was very near death.

00:28:49.630 --> 00:28:53.840
Had I had to wait another
two days for the English,

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:56.560
I would not have survived.

00:28:56.560 --> 00:29:03.340
And, the, it's, I have brooded
on that, that I have not

00:29:03.340 --> 00:29:05.260
been conscious at
what I consider

00:29:05.260 --> 00:29:06.873
to be the most important

00:29:06.873 --> 00:29:08.290
INTERVIEWER 2:
What happened next?

00:29:08.290 --> 00:29:09.230
RENEE H.: experience.

00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:10.960
I came to Sweden.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:18.390
I was given over to a doctor
in Sweden who immediately

00:29:18.390 --> 00:29:20.250
decided to fatten me up.

00:29:20.250 --> 00:29:24.180
Because I weighed at
the age of eleven,

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:26.040
I weighed as much as a
three-year-old child.

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:32.630
And had a big pot belly.

00:29:32.630 --> 00:29:37.880
My sister is alive and she
is, came to New York with me.

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:39.200
She went to a school.

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:41.400
She went to the Lexington
School for the Deaf.

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:42.080
She married.

00:29:42.080 --> 00:29:43.040
She has three children.

00:29:45.980 --> 00:29:49.790
Coming to the United
States was a great shock.

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:55.575
I happened to come
on, in August of 1948.

00:29:55.575 --> 00:30:00.250
It was the hottest day in the
history of the United States.

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:03.800
I had come from Sweden and
I walked out of the plane,

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:08.180
and I remember turning around
and saying, "I'm going back."

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:15.490
Because not only was
it very, very hot,

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:20.070
but at the airport, greeting
this plane which had taken us

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:26.230
24 hours to fly from
Stockholm to New York,

00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:32.590
I was suddenly faced by a
whole panoply of black faces.

00:30:32.590 --> 00:30:36.020
Nobody in Sweden had
told me that there were

00:30:36.020 --> 00:30:39.100
Negroes in the United States.

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:42.630
I knew that they were in
Africa, but to suddenly come

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:46.890
into New York and see
all those black faces,

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:49.652
I was sure I had come
into the wrong country.

00:30:49.652 --> 00:30:51.485
I was supposed to come
to the United States,

00:30:51.485 --> 00:30:56.565
and here was this hot place
with, all the black people.

00:30:56.565 --> 00:30:57.990
INTERVIEWER 1: What country?

00:30:57.990 --> 00:30:58.780
RENEE H.: Par--

00:30:58.780 --> 00:30:59.947
INTERVIEWER 1: What country?

00:30:59.947 --> 00:31:00.520
What place?

00:31:00.520 --> 00:31:00.790
What--

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:01.155
INTERVIEWER 2: New York.

00:31:01.155 --> 00:31:01.520
She--

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:02.437
INTERVIEWER 1: I know.

00:31:02.437 --> 00:31:04.123
But where do you
think you-- you got?

00:31:04.123 --> 00:31:04.790
Where did they--

00:31:04.790 --> 00:31:06.707
RENEE H.: I thought I
was somewhere in Africa,

00:31:06.707 --> 00:31:08.430
I had landed
somewhere in Africa.

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:09.212
It was so hot.

00:31:09.212 --> 00:31:11.587
INTERVIEWER 1: The plane went
to another place this time?

00:31:11.587 --> 00:31:12.561
RENEE H.: That's right.

00:31:12.561 --> 00:31:13.103
That's right.

00:31:13.103 --> 00:31:16.680
It was exactly,
it was, it was my

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:20.910
having in some way sensed
that life was playing tricks

00:31:20.910 --> 00:31:24.570
on me all the time, that I
wasn't going to the places

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:27.150
I thought I was going to.

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:29.700
And then, at the ramp
were my relatives,

00:31:29.700 --> 00:31:32.700
waiting for me,
none of whom I knew.

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:34.320
I had never met them.

00:31:34.320 --> 00:31:38.670
They had all left long
before I was born.

00:31:38.670 --> 00:31:42.290
They were yelling and screaming,
"Renée, Renée, Renée."

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:48.310
So I knew that I was where
I was supposed to be,

00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:51.280
but what was strange was
having to get adjusted.

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:55.680
Not only to a totally different
milieu, but to relatives

00:31:55.680 --> 00:32:01.300
I had absolutely no inkling of.

